{%- comment -%}
  Product card — a 1:1 port of the design's `Product Card.dc.html`.

  The design's card is a single <a> with buttons nested inside it, which is
  neither valid HTML nor navigable. The same behaviour is reached here with the
  overlay-link pattern: the title carries the only link and its ::after covers
  the whole card, so clicking anywhere on the media or the caption opens the
  piece, while the controls sit above that layer and act on their own. One link
  per card, in the tab order once, named by the product.

  Media is a stack of cross-fading slides, not a single hover swap. Every
  photograph the piece has is a slide — one or a hundred, there is no cap —
  led by the media belonging to the variant the card stands for, with the 360°
  spin last. Hovering previews the next photograph, the arrows and a swipe step
  through every slide. Without scripting the first slide is simply the one on
  show, hover still reveals the second, and every card is still a real link.

  Only those first two slides are fetched up front, whatever the count; the
  rest are deferred behind data-src and fetched as the card is stepped to them.

  **This is retained only for the design-system diagnostic template.** Live
  collection, search and Featured products surfaces use
  `blocks/_product-card.liquid`, whose parts are editable theme blocks. Both
  implementations share the `data-card-*` behavior contract so the diagnostic
  remains useful while it exists.

  Accepts:
    product - product object. Renders a placeholder when blank.
    loading - 'lazy' (default) or 'eager'.
    sizes   - sizes attribute. Defaults to the design's own.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign img_loading = loading | default: 'lazy'
  assign img_sizes = sizes | default: '(max-width: 700px) 70vw, 460px'

  comment
    **This card's settings are its own, because there is no longer a "Product
    cards" group in the theme settings.** A card is edited on the card, and the
    card that can be edited is `blocks/_product-card.liquid` — its parts are
    theme blocks with their own controls.

    This snippet carries fixed values only for the design-system diagnostic.
    Storefront product grids are built on the editable card block.
  endcomment
  assign card_image_ratio = '1/1'
  assign card_show_vendor = false
  assign card_show_metal = true
  assign card_show_swatches = true
  assign card_show_options_note = true
  assign card_show_video = true
  assign card_show_add = true
  assign cart_name = settings.cart_name | default: 'Cart'
  assign card_add_label = 'products.add_to_cart' | t: cart_name: cart_name

  comment
    The frame.

    The design's is 3/4 (`aspect-ratio:3/4` on its media div); the **shipped
    default is Square**, on request — see the note in CLAUDE.md. 3/4 is still
    the fallback here, for a card with no photograph to adapt to.

    Every value of the setting has to do what it says, including **Adapt to
    image**, which did not: it fell through to the same 3/4 box, so choosing it
    changed nothing at all. Adapt now takes the shape from the first photograph
    the card actually shows, which is the one the frame has to fit.

    Worth knowing what that means on this catalogue: every photograph here is
    3:2 landscape (2560x1707), the same shape the design's own are. In a 3/4
    frame at `object-fit: contain` a 3:2 photograph fills 58-70% of the height,
    so a band of the card's surface shows above and below the piece. That is the
    design's own arrangement rather than a fault in the port — Adapt is the
    setting that closes the band, by making the frame the photograph's shape.

    A card with no photograph keeps 3/4, since there is nothing to adapt to.
  endcomment
  assign ratio_value = '3 / 4'
  if card_image_ratio == 'adapt'
    assign ratio_media = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'image' | first
    if ratio_media.width > 0 and ratio_media.height > 0
      assign ratio_value = ratio_media.width | append: ' / ' | append: ratio_media.height
    endif
  else
    assign ratio_value = card_image_ratio | replace: '/', ' / '
  endif

  comment
    The metal control the design puts on the card itself. Picking a metal moves
    the caption, the price and what goes in the bag — and deliberately *not* the
    photograph: "Colour is chosen on the card itself: the name, the price and
    what goes in the bag all follow it. The photograph does not — we shoot one
    metal." (Product Card.dc.html.)

    This is the real option, matched on the name alone. The caption's own
    fallback below is looser — it will take a "Gold Purity" option to have
    something to say — but a looser match must never become a control: it would
    put swatches on an option that is not a metal.

    This has to resolve before the caption, the price and the media, all of
    which now read it.
  endcomment
  comment
    Resolved whatever the swatch setting says, because the caption and the price
    both read it. Gating the lookup on the setting coupled them to it: turning
    swatches off silently changed the caption's wording and the price's range.
    The setting decides whether the *row* is rendered, nothing else.
  endcomment
  assign metal_option = blank
  assign metal_index = 0
  for option in product.options_with_values
    assign option_down = option.name | downcase
    if option_down contains 'metal'
      assign metal_option = option
      assign metal_index = forloop.index0
      break
    endif
  endfor
  comment
    The design hides the row unless more than one metal is on show
    (`hasSwatches: swatches.length > 1`, and again when only one fits). This
    theme shows it at one, on request: a single swatch still says what the piece
    is made of, which is worth a row even when there is nothing to choose.
  endcomment
  if metal_option.values.size < 1
    assign metal_option = blank
  endif

  comment
    The design's own metal table decides the order, not the order the option
    happens to carry. It lives in snippets/metal-order.liquid because the quick
    view orders its swatch row the same way — the table is the design's, not
    this card's.
  endcomment
  capture ordered_metals_raw
    render 'metal-order', option: metal_option
  endcapture
  assign ordered_metals = ordered_metals_raw | strip | split: '||'

  assign card_variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant

  comment
    Adding from the card picks the dearest variant, on request — the card
    offers no karat or size control, so it has to choose one. Available first,
    because a sold-out variant cannot be bought; the dearest overall stands in
    when none is, and the button then says Sold out.

    Kept apart from card_variant, whose only job is to say which metal the card
    opens on. Where there is a metal control the swatch loop works this out per
    metal instead; this is the answer for a piece without one.
  endcomment
  assign dearest_variant = blank
  assign dearest_any_variant = blank
  for variant in product.variants
    if dearest_any_variant == blank or variant.price > dearest_any_variant.price
      assign dearest_any_variant = variant
    endif
    if variant.available
      if dearest_variant == blank or variant.price > dearest_variant.price
        assign dearest_variant = variant
      endif
    endif
  endfor
  if dearest_variant == blank
    assign dearest_variant = dearest_any_variant
  endif

  comment
    The caption is the metal and the carat weight: "White Gold · 1.5 ct".

    No karat prefix. It used to read "22K White Gold · 1.5 ct", which said the
    karat twice — the note directly underneath already lists every karat the
    piece comes in ("Available in 18K, 22K"), and the card cannot choose one
    anyway. Nothing here should look up the purity option.
  endcomment
  assign carat_label = product.metafields.custom.total_carat_weight.value | append: ''
  if carat_label != blank
    unless carat_label contains 'ct'
      assign carat_label = carat_label | append: ' ct'
    endunless
  endif

  comment
    The metal line.

    Where the card carries a metal control the option wins, because the caption
    has to follow the pick — the design's whole point — and a metafield holds
    one fixed string that cannot. Every product in this catalogue sets
    custom.metal, so letting it win would have frozen the caption on every card
    in the shop: choose White Gold and it would still have read "18 Karat
    Yellow Gold", which is not a preference but a wrong answer.

    Without a control the metafield still wins, and then the caption is fixed
    and says so, which is the single-metal piece it was written for.
  endcomment
  assign metal = blank
  if card_show_metal
    if metal_option != blank
      assign metal = card_variant.options[metal_index]
      if carat_label != blank
        assign metal = metal | append: ' · ' | append: carat_label
      endif
    else
      for option in product.options_with_values
        assign name_down = option.name | downcase
        if name_down == 'metal'
          assign metal = option.selected_value | default: option.values.first
          break
        endif
      endfor
    endif
  endif

  comment
    The framing correction, from the piece's own metafield and nothing else.

    It used to be a table: "bracelet", "earring" and "ring" matched against the
    product's type and title — earrings before rings, because "earrings"
    contains "ring" — with `custom.card_zoom` in front of it and a theme setting
    turning the whole thing off. One catalogue's categories written into a theme
    that is meant to sell anything, so it went with the settings group.

    `blocks/_product-card-media.liquid` has the better answer: a setting that
    takes a dynamic source, so which metafield holds the figure is the
    merchant's choice. This snippet has nowhere to put one, so the name stays
    hardcoded until these templates are rebuilt on the card block. A piece
    without it gets no correction, where before it got its category's.
  endcomment
  assign card_zoom = product.metafields.custom.card_zoom.value

  comment
    Two balanced lines, split where the halves come closest in length — the
    design's own rule, so every caption in a row occupies exactly two lines and
    the cards stay level. Ties fall to the later split, as the design's does.
  endcomment
  assign title_line_1 = product.title
  assign title_line_2 = ''
  assign words = product.title | strip | split: ' '
  if words.size > 1
    assign best_at = 1
    assign best_gap = 99999
    assign last_k = words.size | minus: 1

    for k in (1..last_k)
      assign left_len = 0
      assign right_len = 0
      for word in words
        if forloop.index0 < k
          assign left_len = left_len | plus: word.size | plus: 1
        else
          assign right_len = right_len | plus: word.size | plus: 1
        endif
      endfor
      assign gap = left_len | minus: right_len
      if gap < 0
        assign gap = 0 | minus: gap
      endif
      if gap <= best_gap
        assign best_gap = gap
        assign best_at = k
      endif
    endfor

    assign title_line_1 = ''
    assign title_line_2 = ''
    for word in words
      if forloop.index0 < best_at
        assign title_line_1 = title_line_1 | append: ' ' | append: word
      else
        assign title_line_2 = title_line_2 | append: ' ' | append: word
      endif
    endfor
    assign title_line_1 = title_line_1 | strip
    assign title_line_2 = title_line_2 | strip
  endif

  comment
    The design's karat note. It is not "the other options" — it is three named
    axes and no others:

      karatNote = [labels('purity',' / '), labels('origin',' or '),
                   labels('sold',' or ')].filter(Boolean).join(' · ')

    so purity joins with a slash while origin and purchase join with the word
    "or", and size, format, cut, backing and clasp are all deliberately absent.
    Listing every option instead put the ring sizes on the card — "18K / Test
    21K · Test Size 6.5 / Test Size 5.5" — which the design never shows.

    The catalogue's "Test " prefixes read verbatim: a theme that rewrote
    catalogue strings would only hide that they need fixing in admin.
  endcomment
  comment
    The note is the karat sentence — "Available in 18K, 22K" — and nothing
    else. Size stays out, as it always has.

    Every value the option carries is listed, whatever the stock: an option
    value exists because the piece can be made that way, and a sold-out karat
    is still a karat the shop offers. Filtering by `available` would make the
    sentence flicker as inventory moved.

    Sorted smallest karat first, numerically. A plain string sort puts "22K"
    before "9K", so the number is pulled out of each value and zero-padded into
    a sortable key, then thrown away again. A value with no number in it sorts
    to the front rather than being dropped.
  endcomment
  assign options_note = ''
  if card_show_options_note
    assign karat_option = blank
    for option in product.options_with_values
      assign option_down = option.name | downcase
      if option_down contains 'purity' or option_down contains 'karat'
        assign karat_option = option
        break
      endif
    endfor

    if karat_option != blank
      assign karat_keys = ''
      for value in karat_option.values
        assign digits = value | downcase | remove: 'karat' | remove: 'kt' | remove: 'k' | strip
        assign number = digits | plus: 0
        assign pad = ''
        if number < 10
          assign pad = '00'
        elsif number < 100
          assign pad = '0'
        endif
        assign karat_keys = karat_keys | append: pad | append: number | append: '~' | append: value | append: '||'
      endfor

      assign karat_keys = karat_keys | split: '||' | sort
      assign karat_list = ''
      for key in karat_keys
        assign label = key | split: '~' | last
        if karat_list == blank
          assign karat_list = label
        else
          assign karat_list = karat_list | append: ', ' | append: label
        endif
      endfor

      if karat_list != blank
        assign options_note = 'products.available_in' | t: list: karat_list
      endif
    endif
  endif

  comment
    Every photograph the piece has, however many that is. Nothing is capped:
    a piece with one and a piece with a hundred both render their whole set,
    and only the first two are fetched up front — see snippets/card-slide.

    The order is the product's own, and the metal control above deliberately
    does not touch it. The design is explicit: "Colour is chosen on the card
    itself: the name, the price and what goes in the bag all follow it. The
    photograph does not — we shoot one metal."

    An earlier pass led the order with the selected variant's own media. It was
    inert — no variant in the shop carries media — but it was aimed the wrong
    way: assigning variant media in admin would have started the opening
    photograph following the metal, which is the one thing the design rules out.
    The metal in a filename is the only metal signal the media carries, and
    nothing here parses filenames.
  endcomment
  assign card_images = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'image'
  assign photo_count = card_images.size

  comment
    The spin counts only once it is known to be playable. Counting the video
    itself put the arrows and the badge on a card whose video has no mp4
    rendition — controls for a slide that is never rendered, because the slide
    itself is guarded on the rendition and the count was not.
  endcomment
  assign card_video = blank
  assign card_mp4 = blank
  if card_show_video
    assign product_videos = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'video'
    assign first_video = product_videos.first
    if first_video != blank
      assign mp4_sources = first_video.sources | where: 'format', 'mp4'
      assign card_mp4 = mp4_sources.last
      if card_mp4 != blank
        assign card_video = first_video
      endif
    endif
  endif

  assign slide_count = photo_count
  if card_video != blank
    assign slide_count = slide_count | plus: 1
  endif

  comment
    Slide 0 is what the card shows and slide 1 is what hover previews. Those
    two are the whole of it without scripting, so those two are what the
    browser fetches.
  endcomment
  assign eager_slides = 2

  assign quick_view_label = 'products.quick_view_of' | t: title: product.title
-%}

{%- if product == blank -%}
  <div class="card">
    <div class="card__media" style="aspect-ratio: {{ ratio_value }};">
      {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'placeholder-svg' }}
    </div>

    <div class="card__body">
      <span class="card__title">
        <span class="card__title-line">{{ 'onboarding.product_title' | t }}</span>
        <span class="card__title-line"></span>
      </span>
      {%- if card_show_metal -%}
        <span class="card__meta"></span>
      {%- endif -%}
      <span class="card__price">
        {{- 1999 | money -}}
      </span>
      {%- if card_show_add -%}
        <span class="btn btn--square card__add" aria-disabled="true">
          {{ card_add_label }}
        </span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    data-card-multi drives the no-JavaScript hover swap only, and that swap can
    only reveal a photograph — the stylesheet excludes a spin from it, since a
    spin has nothing to show until its source is fetched. So it is gated on the
    photograph count, not the slide count: a piece with one photograph and a
    spin used to fade its only photograph out on hover and reveal nothing.
    The arrows are a separate question and stay on the slide count, because the
    script can step to the spin perfectly well.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Both button labels ride on the card so the metal pick can swap between them
    without a translation string in JavaScript — a metal can be sold out while
    the one Liquid rendered was not.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign add_label = card_add_label -%}
  {%- assign sold_out_label = 'products.sold_out' | t -%}
  <div
    class="card"
    data-card
    {% if photo_count > 1 %}data-card-multi{% endif %}
    data-add-label="{{ add_label | escape }}"
    data-sold-out-label="{{ sold_out_label | escape }}"
  >
    <div
      class="card__media"
      style="aspect-ratio: {{ ratio_value }};{% if card_zoom != blank %} --card-zoom: {{ card_zoom }};{% endif %}"
    >
      {%- if slide_count > 0 -%}
        {%- assign slide_i = 0 -%}

        {%- for media in card_images -%}
          {%- assign slide_eager = false -%}
          {%- if slide_i < eager_slides -%}
            {%- assign slide_eager = true -%}
          {%- endif -%}
          {% render 'card-slide',
            media: media,
            index: slide_i,
            eager: slide_eager,
            product: product,
            sizes: img_sizes,
            loading: img_loading
          %}
          {%- assign slide_i = slide_i | plus: 1 -%}
        {%- endfor -%}

        {%- if card_video != blank -%}
          {%- assign card_video_lqip = card_video.preview_image | image_url: width: 40 -%}
          <span
            class="card__slide{% if photo_count == 0 %} is-on{% endif %}"
            data-card-slide="{{ photo_count }}"
            data-card-spin
          >
            <video
              class="card__img card__video"
              data-src="{{ card_mp4.url }}"
              poster="{{ card_video.preview_image | image_url: width: 600 }}"
              width="{{ card_video.preview_image.width | default: 1240 }}"
              height="{{ card_video.preview_image.height | default: 827 }}"
              muted
              loop
              playsinline
              preload="none"
              aria-label="{{ 'products.spin_view' | t }}"
              data-video-lqip="{{ card_video_lqip }}"
            ></video>
            {% render 'video-lqip-frame', lqip: card_video_lqip %}
          </span>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- else -%}
        <span class="card__slide is-on">
          {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'placeholder-svg' }}
        </span>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if settings.quick_view_enabled -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          A real link to the piece, which theme.js upgrades into the overlay.
          Without scripting it lands on the product page — the same information,
          one navigation further away.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <a
          class="card__quick"
          href="{{ product.url }}"
          data-quick-view
          data-tip="{{ 'products.quick_view' | t }}"
          aria-label="{{ quick_view_label }}"
        >
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'eye' %}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if card_video != blank -%}
        <span class="card__spin" data-card-spin-label{% if photo_count > 0 %} hidden{% endif %}>
          {{- 'products.spin_badge' | t -}}
        </span>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if slide_count > 1 -%}
        <button
          class="card__arrow card__arrow--prev"
          type="button"
          data-card-step="-1"
          data-tip="{{ 'products.previous_view' | t }}"
          aria-label="{{ 'products.previous_view' | t }}"
        >
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'chevron-left' %}
        </button>
        <button
          class="card__arrow card__arrow--next"
          type="button"
          data-card-step="1"
          data-tip="{{ 'products.next_view' | t }}"
          aria-label="{{ 'products.next_view' | t }}"
        >
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'chevron-right' %}
        </button>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if product.available == false -%}
        <span class="badge">{{ 'products.sold_out' | t }}</span>
      {%- elsif product.compare_at_price > product.price -%}
        <span class="badge">{{ 'products.on_sale' | t }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    <div class="card__body">
      {%- if card_show_vendor and product.vendor != blank -%}
        <span class="card__vendor">{{ product.vendor }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The only link on the card. Its ::after is what makes the whole card
        clickable, so the media needs no link of its own.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a class="card__title" href="{{ product.url }}" aria-label="{{ product.title | escape }}">
        <span class="card__title-line">{{ title_line_1 }}</span>
        <span class="card__title-line">{{ title_line_2 }}</span>
      </a>

      {%- if card_show_metal -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Without a metal control the caption is a fixed string — the metafield's,
          or a single option's — so it says so and the script leaves it alone.
          With one it has to follow the pick.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span
          class="card__meta"
          data-card-meta
          {% if metal_option == blank %}data-card-meta-fixed{% endif %}
        >{{ metal }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The row is built whenever there is a metal option and only *shown* when
        the setting says so, because the loop below is also where the price
        works out the selected metal's figure. Gating the loop on the setting
        instead left `selected_price_html` unassigned while the price element
        still read it, so turning swatches off emptied the price on every
        product with a metal option — the setting was supposed to decide
        whether the row renders and nothing else.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if metal_option != blank -%}
        {%- assign selected_metal = card_variant.options[metal_index] -%}
        {%- capture metal_row -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Real links to the piece at that metal, so a pick works without
          scripting — it simply navigates — and the script intercepts it.
          They need a z-index of their own: the title's ::after covers the card,
          and a static element paints below a positioned one whatever the
          document order.

          The whole row is rendered. How many fit is a question about this
          card's width rather than the viewport's — in a two-column phone grid
          the card is around 159px and only a couple will sit — so the script
          measures and marks the overflow, and the row simply clips without it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="card__swatches" data-card-swatches>
          {%- for value in ordered_metals -%}
            {%- liquid
              comment
                The card offers no karat control, so adding from it has to pick
                a variant on the customer's behalf: the **dearest** one in this
                metal, on request. Dearest *available* — a sold-out variant
                cannot be bought, so preferring it would post something that
                fails at checkout. Where the whole metal is sold out the dearest
                overall stands in, and the button says Sold out.
              endcomment
              assign chosen = blank
              assign dearest_any = blank
              assign group_min = blank
              assign group_max = blank
              for variant in product.variants
                if variant.options[metal_index] == value
                  if dearest_any == blank or variant.price > dearest_any.price
                    assign dearest_any = variant
                  endif
                  if variant.available
                    if chosen == blank or variant.price > chosen.price
                      assign chosen = variant
                    endif
                  endif
                  if group_min == blank or variant.price < group_min
                    assign group_min = variant.price
                  endif
                  if group_max == blank or variant.price > group_max
                    assign group_max = variant.price
                  endif
                endif
              endfor
              if chosen == blank
                assign chosen = dearest_any
              endif

              comment
                The design's caption is `metalName(sel) + ' · ' + carat`. The
                carat half needs a custom.total_carat_weight metafield, which
                this shop has not created — where it is absent the caption is
                simply the metal, as it was.
              endcomment
              assign metal_label = value
              if carat_label != blank
                assign metal_label = metal_label | append: ' · ' | append: carat_label
              endif

              assign is_selected = false
              if value == selected_metal
                assign is_selected = true
              endif

              comment
                ordered_metals came back through split, so `value` is a plain
                string and no longer the product_option_value drop. The drop is
                what carries a merchant's native Shopify swatch, so it is looked
                up again here — without this the swatch snippet's first and most
                specific branch is silently unreachable.
              endcomment
              assign value_drop = blank
              for candidate in metal_option.values
                if candidate == value
                  assign value_drop = candidate
                  break
                endif
              endfor
            -%}

            {%- capture metal_price_html -%}
              {%- if chosen.compare_at_price > chosen.price -%}
                <s>{{ chosen.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
              {%- endif -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                The card prices what it adds. It used to show "From <cheapest>"
                whenever the metal's variants differed, but the add button now
                posts the dearest — so the customer would have read one figure
                and found another in the bag. The karats it is available in are
                said in words underneath instead, which is what that note is
                for.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {{- chosen.price | money -}}
              {%- comment -%}
                Inside the capture, not after it: this markup is what the script
                writes into [data-card-price] on a metal pick, so a note left
                outside would be wiped by the first swatch click.
              {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- endcapture -%}

            {%- if is_selected -%}
              {%- assign selected_price_html = metal_price_html -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                The piece the button posts, which is this metal's dearest — not
                card_variant, whose job is only to say which metal the card
                opens on.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- assign selected_add_id = chosen.id -%}
            {%- endif -%}

            <a
              class="card__swatch{% if is_selected %} is-selected{% endif %}"
              href="{{ product.url }}{% if product.url contains '?' %}&{% else %}?{% endif %}variant={{ chosen.id }}"
              data-card-metal
              data-metal-value="{{ value | escape }}"
              data-metal-id="{{ chosen.id }}"
              data-metal-available="{{ chosen.available }}"
              data-metal-meta="{{ metal_label | escape }}"
              data-metal-price="{{ metal_price_html | escape }}"
              data-tip="{{ value | escape }}"
              aria-label="{{ value | escape }}"
              aria-current="{% if is_selected %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
              {% if forloop.index0 >= 3 %}hidden{% endif %}
            >
              {%- comment -%}
                `selected` is deliberately not passed. The wrapping link carries
                the state and `.card__swatch.is-selected .swatch-dot` draws the
                ring from it, so there is one source of truth. Marking the dot
                as well left two rings after a pick: the script moves the class
                on the link and never on the dot, so the metal Liquid rendered
                as selected kept its ring for ever. The parameter stays for the
                product page, which uses the dot without a wrapper.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {% render 'metal-swatch', option_value: value_drop, size: 80 %}
            </a>
          {%- endfor -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The design's "+N". Before a width is known it shows three —
            `else if (!avail) n = Math.min(keys.length, 3)` — so that is what
            Liquid renders, and the script re-fits once it can measure.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign metal_total = ordered_metals.size -%}
          <span
            class="card__swatch-more"
            data-card-swatch-more
            {% if metal_total <= 3 %}hidden{% endif %}
          >{% if metal_total > 3 %}+{{ metal_total | minus: 3 }}{% endif %}</span>
        </div>
        {%- endcapture -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The row is captured whatever happens, because the loop inside it is
          also where the price and the add button work out the selected metal's
          variant. Only what is *printed* changes here.

          A metal whose value carries no swatch paints nothing and keeps its
          hairline and its accessible name, which is metal-swatch.liquid's own
          rule: a missing swatch beats an invented one, and the metal stays
          pickable and announced either way.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if card_show_swatches -%}
          {{ metal_row }}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if options_note != blank -%}
        <div class="card__note">{{ options_note }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        `t` has to be handed an already-formatted price: written as
        `t: price: x | money` the money filter takes the *translated string* as
        its input, not the price, and renders nothing at all.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        With a metal control on the card, `price_varies` is the wrong question:
        it is true the moment any two variants differ, including two ring sizes,
        so a piece that costs the same in both metals still collapsed to "From".
        The range that belongs next to a metal swatch is the range *within that
        metal*, which is what the swatch loop worked out.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="card__price" data-card-price>
        {%- if metal_option != blank -%}
          {{- selected_price_html -}}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- if dearest_variant.compare_at_price > dearest_variant.price -%}
            <s>{{ dearest_variant.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
          {%- endif -%}
          {{- dearest_variant.price | money -}}
        {%- endif -%}
      </span>

      {%- if card_show_add -%}
        {%- if product.available -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            A plain product form, so adding works without scripting. theme.js
            intercepts it when the bag drawer is on and posts it in the
            background instead, then slides the drawer out.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- form 'product', product, class: 'card__add-form', data-cart-add: '' -%}
            <input
              type="hidden"
              name="id"
              value="{{ selected_add_id | default: dearest_variant.id }}"
              data-card-add-id
            >
            <button type="submit" name="add" class="btn btn--square card__add">
              {{ card_add_label }}
            </button>
          {%- endform -%}
        {%- else -%}
          <span class="btn btn--square card__add" aria-disabled="true">
            {{ 'products.sold_out' | t }}
          </span>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
